  "prompt_text": "This figure is a schematic map of many membrane-less cellular compartments distributed across a neuron-like cell, linking each structure to its main biological role. It emphasizes that distinct condensates or granules appear in different regions—cell body, nucleus, dendrite-like branches, and the synaptic terminal—and specialize in processes such as RNA handling, signaling, transport, and gene control.\n\nAround the cytoplasm are examples like stress granules, transport granules, P bodies/U bodies, SEC bodies, and TIS granules, which are associated with RNA storage, processing, intracellular transport, or protein complex assembly. Other localized structures include signalling clusters, centrosomes, virus factories, and synaptic vesicle clusters, reflecting roles in cell-cell communication, cell division, viral replication, and vesicle organization.\n\nInside the nucleus, several bodies are shown surrounding or within the nuclear region, including the nucleolus, nuclear speckles, paraspeckles, Cajal bodies, heterochromatin, transcription foci, and nuclear pores. These are tied to ribosome production, RNA processing and splicing, transcriptional regulation, gene regulation, and transport across the nuclear envelope. Germ granules are also highlighted as a specialized class involved in germ cell specification and storage of RNA and proteins.\n\nOverall, the image conveys a spatial overview of functionally diverse biomolecular condensates in cells.",
